        <b:Id>70</b:Id>
        <b:InteractionKey>9ad86cf2-fcc4-43dc-9c0d-ad1adda9cad3:1bef0ff4-65ae-49f7-a9dc-10d72e3b6edc:ee45e66c-433d-4dd9-af86-97260e5c54a8</b:InteractionKey>
        <b:InteractionString i:nil="true"/>
        <b:ModifiedAt>2026-09-10T05:18:09Z</b:ModifiedAt>
        <b:Priority>High</b:Priority>
        <b:Properties>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Title</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Potential Excessive Resource Consumption for Authentication engine + MSAL or Platform-secure reusable state</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatCategory</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Denial Of Service</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatShortDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Denial of Service happens when the process or a datastore is not able to service incoming requests or perform up to spec.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>UserThreatDescription</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Does Authentication engine + MSAL or Platform-secure reusable state take explicit steps to control resource consumption? Resource consumption attacks can be hard to deal with, and there are times that it makes sense to let the OS do the job. Be careful that your resource requests don't deadlock, and that they do timeout.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>InteractionString</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Secure state update</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>Priority</a:Key>
            <a:Value>High</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
          <a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
            <a:Key>StateInformation</a:Key>
            <a:Value>Design review only; no runtime mitigation is claimed. See docs/security/threat-model.md#tmt-analysis-and-disposition. Keep state coordination and retries under the request deadline; shared-state integrity does not require an unbounded service or cross-process interaction single-flight. V2-REQ-015, V2-REQ-025, V2-REQ-041.</a:Value>
          </a:KeyValueOfstringstring>
        </b:Properties>
        <b:SourceGuid>9ad86cf2-fcc4-43dc-9c0d-ad1adda9cad3</b:SourceGuid>
        <b:State>NeedsInvestigation</b:State>
        <b:StateInformation>Design review only; no runtime mitigation is claimed. See docs/security/threat-model.md#tmt-analysis-and-disposition. Keep state coordination and retries under the request deadline; shared-state integrity does not require an unbounded service or cross-process interaction single-flight. V2-REQ-015, V2-REQ-025, V2-REQ-041.</b:StateInformation>
        <b:TargetGuid>ee45e66c-433d-4dd9-af86-97260e5c54a8</b:TargetGuid>
